CAT = "cpaiscSocPlan"; SEG = "SPL"
Q = [
("The common criteria of the Trust Services Criteria are aligned with:",
 "COSO's 17 principles", ["NIST CSF 2.0's six Functions", "COBIT 2019's governance objectives", "The 18 CIS Controls"],
 "The TSC are aligned to the 17 principles of the 2013 COSO Internal Control - Integrated Framework, with supplemental criteria added for trust services engagements."),
("The supplemental criteria in the Trust Services Criteria, such as logical access and change management, expand on which COSO principle?",
 "Principle 12, policies and procedures", ["Principle 1, integrity and ethics", "Principle 7, identifying risks", "Principle 16, ongoing evaluations"],
 "The TSC add supplemental criteria to COSO principle 12, which deploys control activities through policies and procedures. They cover access, system operations, change management and risk mitigation."),
("A SOC 2 examination covers only the security category. Which criteria apply?",
 "Common criteria alone", ["Common criteria plus the A series", "The A, C and PI series", "COSO principles 1 to 17 only"],
 "The common criteria are complete for security, and no extra category criteria are needed. Availability, confidentiality, processing integrity and privacy each add their own series."),
("Which engagement uses the trust services criteria for security, availability and confidentiality as control criteria for an entity's cybersecurity risk management program?",
 "SOC for Cybersecurity", ["SOC 1 examination", "SOC for Supply Chain", "Compliance attestation"],
 "The TSC name the SOC for Cybersecurity examination as using the security, availability and confidentiality criteria. SOC 1 uses control objectives relevant to financial reporting instead."),
("A SOC 1 report is mainly intended for:",
 "User entities and user auditors", ["The general public", "Prospective investors", "Industry regulators"],
 "A SOC 1 report addresses controls relevant to user entities' internal control over financial reporting and is restricted to management, user entities and their auditors."),
("A service organization wants a report on its controls that it can post on its website for anyone to read. Which report fits?",
 "SOC 3", ["SOC 2 type 2", "SOC 2 type 1", "SOC 1 type 2"],
 "A SOC 3 report gives an opinion without the detailed tests and results, so it is meant for general use. SOC 1 and SOC 2 reports are restricted to specified users."),
("In a type 1 report, management makes no assertion that its controls:",
 "Operated effectively", ["Were suitably designed", "Were fairly described", "Were in place at a date"],
 "A type 1 report covers the description and the suitability of design as of a date. Operating effectiveness over a period is added only in a type 2 report."),
("Under AT-C 320, a service auditor does not need to be independent of:",
 "Each user entity", ["The service organization", "An inclusive-method subservice organization", "Service organization management"],
 "AT-C 320 states the service auditor need not be independent of each user entity, since there may be hundreds. Independence from the service organization is required."),
("In a SOC 1 engagement, materiality relates to:",
 "The information being reported on", ["User entities' financial statements", "The service organization's revenue", "The total number of deviations"],
 "AT-C 320 says materiality relates to the information reported on, not the user entities' financial statements: the description, the design of controls and their operating effectiveness."),
("For the fair presentation of the description and the design of controls, materiality in a SOC 1 engagement mainly considers:",
 "Qualitative factors", ["A percentage of revenue", "Observed deviation rates", "User entity net income"],
 "AT-C 320 says materiality for the description and design primarily involves qualitative factors, such as whether the description omits or distorts relevant information. Deviation rates matter for operating effectiveness."),
("A service organization uses another company's data center to run the payroll system whose controls matter to user entities' financial reporting. The data center company is a:",
 "Subservice organization", ["Downstream user entity", "Related-party service vendor", "Business associate"],
 "A subservice organization is a service organization used by another service organization to perform services relevant to user entities' ICFR. User entities are the customers."),
("Under the carve-out method, the subservice organization's relevant control objectives and controls are:",
 "Excluded from description and scope", ["Included and tested by the auditor", "Tested but left out of the description", "Described but not tested at all"],
 "Under the carve-out method the description names the services the subservice organization performs but leaves its controls out of the description and the engagement scope."),
("When the inclusive method is used, the service auditor must also obtain written representations from:",
 "Subservice management", ["User entity management", "The user auditors", "The subservice's own auditor"],
 "AT-C 320 requires the same written representations from management of a subservice organization presented using the inclusive method."),
("Controls that service organization management assumes, in designing its system, will be implemented by a subservice organization are called:",
 "CSOCs", ["CUECs", "Compensating controls", "Entity-level controls"],
 "Complementary subservice organization controls are assumed at the subservice organization. Complementary user entity controls are assumed at user entities."),
("Which is a typical complementary user entity control for a company that uses a payroll service organization?",
 "User approves the pay changes it submits", ["Provider approves pay changes it receives", "Provider restricts data center entry", "Provider tests its recovery plan"],
 "CUECs are controls the user entity must operate for the service organization's control objectives to be met, such as authorizing the changes it sends. The others are the provider's own controls."),
("In a SOC 2 examination, promises a service organization makes to customers in contracts and service level agreements are its:",
 "Service commitments", ["System requirements", "Control objectives", "Points of focus"],
 "The TSC call declarations made to customers about system performance commitments. System requirements describe how the system must function to meet those commitments and laws."),
("In a SOC 2 examination, specifications of how the system should function to meet commitments, laws and regulations are called:",
 "System requirements", ["Service commitments", "Description criteria", "Control objectives"],
 "System requirements are how the system should function to achieve commitments to customers, relevant laws and regulations, and industry guidelines."),
("Must a SOC 2 examination show that every point of focus in the Trust Services Criteria is addressed?",
 "No, they are not required", ["Yes, every point must be met", "Only those for privacy", "Only in a type 2 examination"],
 "The TSC state that using the criteria does not require an assessment of whether each point of focus is addressed. Points of focus help in applying the criteria."),
("Under AT-C 320, who is responsible for identifying the risks that threaten the control objectives?",
 "Service organization management", ["The service auditor's engagement team", "User entity management", "The user auditor"],
 "Management acknowledges responsibility for identifying those risks and designing controls to address them. The service auditor assesses management's process."),
("After the report date, what responsibility does a service auditor have regarding the subject matter?",
 "No procedures, but respond to facts", ["Monitor the subject matter until next report", "Repeat tests for 90 more days", "Retest all controls each quarter"],
 "AT-C 205 says the practitioner need not perform procedures after the report date but should respond appropriately to facts later learned that might have changed the report."),
]
